  "Question": "Suppose that \\(|x + y| + |x - y| = 2.\\) What is the maximum possible value of \\(x^2 - 6x + y^2?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(5\\)",
    "B": "\\(6\\)",
    "C": "\\(7\\)",
    "D": "\\(8\\)",
    "E": "\\(9\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "The useful observation is\n\\[\n|x+y|+|x-y|=2\\max(|x|,|y|).\n\\]\nTo verify it, suppose first that \\(|x|\\ge |y|\\). Then \\(x+y\\) and \\(x-y\\) have the same sign as \\(x\\), allowing zero, so their absolute values sum to \\(2|x|\\). If \\(|y|\\ge |x|\\), interchange \\(x\\) and \\(y\\); the left side is unchanged, and the sum is \\(2|y|\\).\n\nThe given equation therefore implies \\(\\max(|x|,|y|)=1\\). In particular,\n\\[\nx^2\\le1,\\qquad y^2\\le1,\\qquad -6x\\le6.\n\\]\nAdding these bounds gives\n\\[\nx^2-6x+y^2\\le1+6+1=8.\n\\]\nEquality requires \\(x=-1\\) and \\(y=\\pm1\\). Both pairs satisfy the original absolute-value equation, so the bound is attained. The maximum is \\(\\boxed{8}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{D}\\).",
